  ],
  "text": "Before refrigeration, the most valuable technology on Earth was stopping food from rotting. A harvest you cannot store is not wealth. It is a deadline. Salt came first. It pulls the water out, and microbes cannot work without water. Drying does the same job with sun and wind, and costs nothing at all. Smoke adds chemicals that kill microbes and seals the surface at the same time. Sugar and vinegar work by making the food somewhere nothing wants to live. Then Napoleon offered a prize for feeding an army on the move, and canning was invented to win it. Refrigeration came last, and it rewrote what a city could eat. Every one of them buys exactly the same thing. Time. And a surplus that keeps is what lets somebody stop farming and do something else entirely. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
